# Browser export acceptance

Tested clean source: `b1747ae23886a78ad1b09607356c0a6bb8d3302a`.

The real application ran through `acceptance/compose.yaml`: Spring, PostgreSQL 17, the production Next.js build, Caddy, a fresh synthetic remote Git repository and native Linux managed originals. The stack used an isolated Docker Compose project and loopback origin. A disposable owner session was authenticated through the real HTTP API before recording. No external content repository or model was used.

Chrome 153.0.8010.36, viewport 1440 x 1050. `export-flow.webm` is one continuous browser recording. `export-flow.gif` samples that recording at two frames per second and displays it at 1152 x 840; no separate runs or staged screenshots were spliced in. The GIF and decoded video frames were visually inspected for legibility and sensitive content.

The recording shows file export cancellation and focus restoration, public ZIP creation with unsaved editor text retained, native browser downloads, private selection refused in public scope, successful recovery by choosing a private copy, and folder/workspace selection cancellation. Independent ZIP inspection verifies actual attachment bytes, package-relative links, public metadata removal and exclusion of unsaved text. `checks.json` contains the results and artifact hashes.

Both downloads completed without a browser download error. This local HTTP fixture does not prove final production HTTPS, actual-user corpus behavior, or CLI export. The fixture containers and volumes were removed after acceptance; no test services remain.
